# Community Update #0.1 // Blink Twice

Hi there. My name is Misha, I’m slowly but steadily building an open source venture design methodology and today’s not Tuesday. Setting exact dates for delivering something not related to my primary income source is both not my strongest suit and not a great idea in general. Reminds me of Valve Time. Have you ever heard of Valve Time? Forgive me a brief but amusing offtopic

Valve is a well-known a game development studio responsible for the Half Life series, Counter Strike, Dota 2 and many other titles which, by general consensus, made a huge impact on the industry. Although Valve haven’t released anything particularly groundbreaking over the last few years, the company is still seen as revolutionary and consistent quality-wise. Valve just don’t make bad games. Problem is, they also just don’t release things on time. 

I never really bothered to research if Valve’s unpunctuality is just a result of a disconnect between marketing and production or there’s other forces involved, but the company seeming disability to hold their commitments became a rich soil for memes within the gamer community. So rich that Valve themselves acknowledged this by creating [this wiki page](https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/Valve_Time). 

The concept got the name “Valve Time”: a new unit of measurement. If Valve promises to deliver something in 10 days, it’s safe to assume they meant Valve days so you need to make a conversion to regular days to calculate the real release date. Last time I checked, the Valve Time multiplier was around x1.5 but it’s a living thing, you know. 

Why am I bringing this? Well, it might be time to introduce Misha Time (not intended). Not only it will allow me to turn failures of meeting commitments into a joke, but it also makes me sound like MC Hammer. Win-win. I’m not promising anything though. You know what happens when I do. 

Aight. It’s getting late. Chop chop, venture design. Welcome to Community Update #0.1

## Database
Here’s state of play as of 14 July 2021: 
- [Ventures database](https://airtable.com/shr2EnSngyN2t8hVz): 57 Problems, 80 ideas, 8 ventures (some of them still lack description which I’ll fix soon)
- [Methods database](https://airtable.com/shr0gEdWlOlTWl9A4): 19 Stages, 3 Methods (I’m yet to add validation methods from my last post), 4 metrics (we’re yet to get to those)
- [Reports database](https://airtable.com/shrgR62D4eeIrca2C), 4 reports and 3 Methods Stats entries. 

Feel flee to play around, it’s read-only. As of now the bases are not extremely populated though, so it might be not that interesting. We’ll get there. One thing I’m encouraging you to do though (no pressure!) is to use [New Idea](https://airtable.com/shrqhHmZWQlYwnyzO) and [New Problem](https://airtable.com/shrQMHtuSUqsp7erh) links. No quality or other requirements whatsoever but if you think you have an interesting problem for me to work on or a great startup idea, these links are a great way to make it happen while having to do absolutely nothing and still getting credit. And don’t worry that your idea or problem might get lost in a huge pile: I get email notifications for every submitted form. So don’t be a stranger. Your sacrifice will be forgotten. 

## Current affairs 
Validation time! By using a 1d6 dice (it was 6 ventures in the base then) I have officially decided to validate Jazilla first. in case you’ve forgotten, here’s the current description:

> AI Generating missing parts (Melody, Harmony, Drums or any other requested part of the track) for the musician to take inspiration from, based on the uploaded Ableton file with the unfinished track

![](https://www.erikov.me/content/images/2021/06/image-1.png)

We know that the problem we’re trying to solve (not being able to progress songs beyond the [8-bar-loop stage](https://www.google.com/search?client=safari&rls=en&q=safari+copy+google+search+url&ie=UTF-8&oe=UTF-8), but we still need to validate if users are willing to trust the AI to do this for them. Additionally, we want to gather potential users’ contact details and conduct further interviews and surveys to help us shape the future product. 

For this one we’ll go for the landing page method, meaning I’ll have to buy a domain name as well as design and build a one-pager. I’ll share the link with y’all when it’s live. 

As far as driving the traffic goes, I haven’t decided which advertising platform I’m going to use yet. I’ve also budgeted $200 on paid ads this month for now and I’m not sure if I’ll need to increase the monthly spend. It’s been a while since I’ve personally done any performance marketing, so I’m rusty with CPC benchmarks. All findings, metrics and costs will be documented in the Process database and described in a subsequent Community Update as soon as I call the experiment done. 

## Future
As I’ve mentioned in the previous one, I have big plans regarding this community. Today though we’re just a few and I anticipate the engagement rate to be quite low in the start, so I’m going to wait at least a few weeks before we get to any interactive elements. Enjoy the quiet while it lasts. 

I’ve stressed this already but doesn’t hurt to repeat: being subscribed to this newsletter does involve receiving badly worded call-to-actions but does not oblige you to click anything. Still, if you don’t want to receive community updates like this one and are only interested in the bi-monthly articles, you can unsubscribe by this link. You will still get the newsletter, but no community updates after that. [Here’s the link](https://9iuq00muz4w.typeform.com/to/tapYsaN5). No hard feelings.

That concludes our first proper Community Update. Don’t get married to the format: it might and will be changed. Any feedback that doesn’t require unsubscribing you can direct [here](mailto:erikov@hey.com). Replying to this email also works, as usual. I’ll be happy to chat. 

Oh, a bird has whispered that the newsletter might also not arrive tomorrow but later this week. Who the hell knows. Damn Misha Time 🤷

My name is Misha and you’ll hear from me soon. 

Stay awesome. Stay safe. Stay tuned. 


